NAME
    search — Search for files and directories, because sometimes you
    have absolutely no idea where you put something.

SYNOPSIS
    search <pattern> [path]

DESCRIPTION
    The search command recursively searches through a directory for files
    and directories matching the specified pattern.

    If no path is provided, the search starts from the workspace directory.

    Patterns use wildcard matching, allowing you to search for names
    without knowing the exact filename.

USAGE
    > search <pattern>          # Searches the workspace.
    > search <pattern> <path>   # Searches from the specified path.

FUN FACTS
    - search can look through directories inside directories inside
    directories. It has patience.
    - Wildcards let you search without knowing the complete name.
    - Searching is usually faster than remembering where you put things.
    - If search finds nothing, it is either broken or you really did
    lose the file.

EXAMPLES
    > search *.txt
        Finds all files and directories ending with .txt in the
        workspace.

    > search notes*
        Finds files and directories whose names start with notes.

    > search *.py /home/user
        Searches for Python files starting from /home/user.

    > search config /home/user/projects
        Searches the projects directory for entries matching config.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), cd(1), find(1), man(1)

    Remember, search is what you use when your file exists,
    you just have no idea where.

